tning we took  required heat breaks  the people measuring heat index  and you know telling you you've got to  take 10 minutes you've got to take 15  you've got to get out of the Sun  um people people you know really  suffering under really difficult  conditions  let it be said that no matter how  difficult these conditions were and how  much we felt like you know really put  upon  think of the story think of the people  in the story and you realize your  Situation's not that bad  um something I want to talk about is the  opening it's establishing tone  establishing environment  um maybe with Marcelo if you could talk  about that first cue we quickly this the  opening shot coming down into the cotton  field and the first thing we start to  hear is the workers and obviously this  first cue that you introduce talk about  the tone and the choice of  instrumentation that you have  um Antoine um one of the score to  reflect the spirituality of Peter that  was very important but also  the environment that he's in and this  dual spirituality that is both of his  African origins and also his Christian  faith and the music had to kind of like  do both things and I think in that  opening  we we were trying to establish that  sound of the mystery of the forest  there's a sense that Peter kind of  communicates with with the forest and  the forest kind of helps him you know he  he has kind of a  uh very  uh special connection with nature and  and I used a lot of the bimbo which is a  Brazilian instrument it's the first  instrument you hear it sounds like a  guitar and it's a it's a fighting  instrument from Africa that is  associated with uh the Capoeira fight  and I remember  um when I played this for Antoine  originally he was he didn't know what it  was and I didn't want to say what it was  and I just wanted to see if the sound  would do it and it really did him and  Coronet were both very taken with the  sound and and then the voices were very  important  um as well  both the solo sort of like this African  singer we had this wonderful Joe Virgil  incredible uh  soloist who sings a lot of the in a lot  of the the movie but also  um a classical choir and I was having  this again the dichotomy of  his  ancestor spirituality and and the  Christian and also in some ways the  Christian the way I saw it was both a  relief and a source of inspiration for  him to get back to his family but we at  certain points the music kind of shows a  little bit of the oppression as well of  of European culture and and all of the  things that it was uh  sort of  enforcing on that horrible situation  yeah I love for Mandel and David talk  about these the Work Camp because once  we get there we are in the middle uh  we're taking the perspective of of this  in very harsh very dangerous scenario  there's bombs going off in the  background there's you know hammers  hammering railroad tracks it's not a  quiet peaceful situation you guys carry  that tension especially  for a very long time it's a long  sequence and can you guys talk about  building introducing Dynamics in your  track and introducing the environment  because it's their subtleties and  nuances that I feel like especially in  here like you hear everything you can't  escape it  uh yeah well when we first saw the  images of the labor camp we we knew that  it was going to be a large undertaking  um there wasn't a lot of uh production  sound of the environment to be had uh  with Ed capturing the dialogue really  well but just not um we didn't have a  lot of the movement so that all had to  be filled in and with such a wide image  and so much happening in there uh uh we  knew it was going to be a lot of work to  sort of  um address all the detail  um and so but we had a lot to work with  in  um you know all the conditions that uh  Ed had mentioned on set Antoine wanted  us to bring that to the labor camp he  wanted it to be hot and exhausting not  just for uh the the forced laborers but  also for their uh uh the uh the slave  masters as well  um it was just a miserable experience  for for everybody there or it was hot it  was people were just labor labor labor  um uh uh you know the the bombs going  off in the background implied you know  that they're continuing to work even  beyond the scope of the vision their  their their uh being pressured to get  that railroad done in time uh because  the weapons are are coming to uh  reinforce the the war effort  in addition to that Antoine really  wanted the sound to ground the film in  this gritty  uh  unrelenting reality  and so we also tried to add voices to it  and and we quickly learned that uh too  many voices made it too familiar  and so we started removing voices and we  we still had the work and the labor  efforts  um and in the occasional uh slave master  kind of  exerting their Force but it became much  more tense and unsafe and Dangerous by  removing the human voice  and something that had come up was of  the introduction of the preacher  character it's a it's it's a quick  moment in the beginning but then it was  something that had come up that you saw  an opportunity to kind of accentuate  right later on or to capture those  tracks uh the actor Clyde Clyde Jones  right a really good choice I'm gonna  give you credit just just because I can  so Clyde was hired to be the preacher  and he appeared really only one day he  was scheduled as a as a one-day actor  and he added such a spark  um to the to the Ensemble of that was in  the in the Work Camp that they extended  his contract  um and they gave him you know they put  him in more shots and I'd look in my my  monitor and I go oh he's in the Middle  Ground oh wait now he's in the  background oh he's over here but they  didn't give him anything to say and I  realized that that element of what he's  all about is missing  um so I kidnapped him  uh I saw that he had earmarked and  bookmarked in his prop Bible a whole  range of biblical passages and I so I  put him into a into a four by four  off-road vehicle went about a mile away  from the set and generators and noise  and shooting crude all this I said read  all read everything you got everything  that you think is is Meaningful and he  did and you know he bellowed it out as  if he was in the Work Camp and we  recorded uh not only that day but the  next morning at breakfast  um he said hey I got some more  um  4 30 in the morning okay we'll get it  we'll get it so we got him a second time  and I know we Conrad you know the editor  was quite pleased to have all this  material as wild track and you guys use  some of it I guess oh you used quite a  bit and ultimately it became an  important addition that he was uh in his  own way  um trying to make it more palatable for  the slaves that this is God's doing this  is God's work this is a noble thing to  serve their masters and so at some point  we we couldn't feature it it wasn't to  be uh you know focused on but it was  always going to be on the wind and it  was put in several times at various  levels he's way out there or he's just  around the corner but he was still there  and it was kind of an effective tool to  say that not only were there whips that  would keep the slaves down but it was  also this sort of psychological  manipulation and so that was that was  good stuff that's very effective yeah  there's so much to talk about on this  film there's so much thought in detail  and and you know I think for audiences  to continue to talk about the importance  of this message I think is crucial and  obviously it's I think listening to  Antoine talk about his film why he made  it why it's important to make films that  continue back to this topic I'd love for  Heather to close out for you what was  your takeaway of of of the subject  matter of working with Antoine and just  you know reflecting on the project yes I  for for me what was most important was  that stories like this continue to be  told especially in a time in which you  see school systems trying to remove the  accurate you know  portrayal of history from textbooks in  the curriculum in general with critical  race Theory discussions going on and  that's dangerous because children like  my own who are born you know in recent  years they may think that American  history is having a black president and  Barack Obama but that was quite an  achievement that took several hundreds  of years to accomplish and they need to  know the truth of American history for  African-American children they need to  know how strong their ancestors are and  what I love about this film is that you  see a black man saving himself saving  him family you see him as a hero you see  him as a Survivor and that to me gave me  great inspiration once I read the script  and when I was on set that was the  importance of making this movie  um and working with Antoine and will  what I loved was that they decided early  on that we were going to focus on the  faith of Peter that was a decision that  they made after this script was done  when they sat down and they had  conversations about how did this man  continue to fight how did he continue  his struggle through the swamps with all  these people chasing him and trying to  survive the environments it had to be  his faith in God if anything else and  his love for his family and so even  though you see the brutality of slavery  there's also this really important  message about faith and your love for  family and how that can help you endure  anything  uh so well put thank you guys so much  for coming out tonight I really  appreciate your time thank you all for  coming out and uh thank you yeah tell  your friends it's on Apple TV thank you

